--- B#5: DuplicateParty through the CURRENT (post-fix, post-orchestration-refactor) settleBestCycle ---
Scripts: app/scripts/campaign-05-duplicateparty-postrefactor.mts,
         app/scripts/campaign-05b-decode-revert.mts (both deleted after use)
Signer: DCW. Shape: A->B->A->B->A (SET-16's exact shape) -- 4 distinct-edge invoices between 2
distinct parties (A->B nonce1, B->A nonce1, A->B nonce2, B->A nonce2), all registered live:
  A->B n1: 0x8f9b016c4099cd61b83b9a572afddc6b3f487062afc57635b2d59dcdf9368ef7
  B->A n1: 0x36b1df7407233f06305b053142c7eb1fad1dfc1f7e77015b81bd38a868006ba7
  A->B n2: 0xe2d644c5327bd51c467200a96e99acea2983a6ab68508bdeb9f0d87aa54bed1e
  B->A n2: 0x9db9e978c5a0266196313445c3cfe89f4b7dc55c04e051cd976ddbcca1f20cdd
The solver DID propose a cycle over these four (wNet=1,000,000) -- confirming the solver's own
closed-walk-of-distinct-edges design allows this shape through at the TypeScript layer, exactly as
recorded in plans/04-solver-package.md. settleBestCycle's DCW submission failed with Circle's
generic "FAILED: ESTIMATION_ERROR" (same generic-error problem as the 2026-09-19 nonce bug) -- so
the exact same proposal (invoiceIds, wNet) was re-encoded and replayed via
`cast call settler settle(...) --from <DCW wallet>` directly against the live contract:
  revert data: 0x4bd17b81000000000000000000000000381bc4ada838bffa17d8f5d2945b4b0dd4bfafff
  0x4bd17b81 confirmed via `cast sig "DuplicateParty(address)"` to be exactly that selector; the
  trailing address (0x381bc4ada838bffa17d8f5d2945b4b0dd4bfafff) is one of the two generated test
  parties.
RESULT: CONFIRMED -- the 2026-09-18 DuplicateParty fix still holds when reached through the
current OperatorSigner-dispatching settleBestCycle/DCW-contract-execution path, not just the raw
cast script that originally proved the fix live. This specific combination (fix x orchestration
refactor) had never been checked before this campaign.

--- B#6: insufficient-balance error shapes (Swap Kit + Unified Balance) ---
Unified Balance: not re-triggered -- already hit and documented live, 2026-09-19 ("DCW wallet
funded on a second chain" entry): `spendOntoArc` surfaced `BALANCE_INSUFFICIENT_TOKEN` when the
confirmed Gateway balance (~1.5 USDC at the time) was less than the requested spend + ~1.1 USDC fee.
Cited, not reproduced.

Swap Kit: the plan assumed this error shape was "already seen live during earlier proofs" -- IT
WAS NOT. Grepped this file's own history and found no prior Swap Kit insufficient-balance mention
anywhere in AGENTS.md or plans/06-swap-kit.md. This was therefore a genuine new live check, not a
citation:
  1. `estimateUsdcToEurcSwap` for 999,999 USDC (DCW wallet holds ~9.4 USDC) SUCCEEDED with a real
     quote (app/scripts/campaign-04-swap-insufficient.mts, deleted after use) -- confirms
     estimate/quote is balance-agnostic, does not itself validate against the wallet's holdings.
  2. A real `swapUsdcToEurc` for 15 USDC (wallet holds ~9.40 USDC) via the DCW-backed kit
     (app/scripts/campaign-06-swap-insufficient-execute.mts, deleted after use) threw:
       "Insufficient token balance on Arc Testnet"
     Confirmed via `cast call balanceOf` that the DCW wallet's Arc USDC balance was unchanged
     (9,404,944 base units before and after) -- the rejection happened before any funds moved.
REAL FINDING for AGENTS.md: Swap Kit's insufficient-balance check only fires on the real `swap()`
call, not on `estimateSwap` -- a UI building a pre-submit balance check against the estimate alone
would not catch this; it must check the wallet's actual balance separately, or attempt-and-catch.

Receipt tiles (computeDashboardTiles, from A#3's real SettleResult, wNet=1,000,000, 3-invoice
