================================================================================
SUMMARY
================================================================================
6/6 planned checks executed, all live, all independently verified via `cast`:
  A#1 fundResidualViaGateway (DCW)      -- PASS (real mint, verified)
  A#2 compliance rejection (live)       -- PASS (invoice confirmed never registered)
  A#3 full narrative (both signers)     -- PASS (register x3, settle, quote, fund-residual, all real)
  B#4 NonceNotSequential                -- cited (already on record, 2026-09-19)
  B#5 DuplicateParty post-refactor      -- PASS (fix confirmed to survive the refactor)
  B#6 insufficient-balance (both kits)  -- PASS (Unified Balance cited; Swap Kit newly proven live)

Zero contract bugs. Zero application-code bugs. Two real infrastructure findings, both about
`fundResidualViaGateway`'s robustness rather than its correctness when nothing fails:
  1. The raw-key signer path has an RPC-availability dependency the DCW path does not (raw-key
     signs+broadcasts client-side via viem against a public RPC; DCW routes entirely through
     Circle's backend).
  2. `fundResidualViaGateway` is not safely re-callable as a whole once its deposit leg has
     already succeeded -- a caller must track deposit-success separately, or the function should
     grow a "resume from polling" mode, to avoid a compounding double-deposit on retry. Neither
     finding blocks anything -- both were worked around live in this campaign with a
     poll-then-spend continuation script, and are recorded here as real follow-up material, not
     fixed as part of this campaign (a design change is out of scope for a live-proof campaign).
One real gap in the plan itself was also found and closed: its assumption that Swap Kit's
insufficient-balance error shape was "already seen live" was wrong -- it hadn't been, so B#6
became a genuine new live check for that half, not a citation, and turned up that `estimateSwap`
is balance-agnostic (only the real `swap()` call validates against actual holdings).
